I read all five documents in scope. No source code, no collab logs, no edits.

## Findings, most severe first

**HIGH | Lapse is a conclusion, lines 437-445 | "The judge is composed inside the boundary that possesses the resource... which is the one act the governing document allows a boundary to take beyond appending."**
This misreads the contract it cites. The clause in dao.stream.md (lines 407-410) licenses a boundary that can no longer deposit to close its own resource, and says in the same breath that the boundary "interprets nothing" and that the close "is not a judgement about any event". A reclaim is a judgement. Placing the judge inside the boundary puts an interpreter in the one layer both governing documents say must not interpret, and it contradicts the document's own claim at lines 676-684 that "no layer here interprets what it also executes". Correction: the judge is an ordinary interpreter composed beside the boundary. It holds the closable handle, or a composition-supplied reclaim function, exactly as dao.stream.md lines 385-393 permit an interpreter to receive a transport-owned operation as an argument. Its reclaim is `close!` on a handle it holds, not a boundary privilege. Delete the "observably gone" citation.

**HIGH | Judging lapse, lines 597-608 | The cap paragraph contradicts itself.**
Line 598 says max caps tenure "within one uninterrupted judge lifetime; every restart resets the measurement". Line 607 says "In all three cases max still bounds tenure, because tenure start is set once at the grant and no gap or restart moves it." Both cannot hold. Under persisted terms the tenure start survives and the cap holds across restarts. Under re-grant every restart mints a new lease with a new tenure start, so the cap bounds the lease and never the resource. Correction: state that the cap survives restarts only where the terms are persisted, that under re-grant the resource is uncapped across restarts, and that a gap with state intact does not move the tenure start.

**HIGH | Out of scope, lines 790-807 | "Cancellation is what a lapse is... stronger than a cancellation flag."**
The generalization is sound in one role assignment and wrong in the one the document illustrates. Non-renewal cancels only when the canceller is the holder and the activity being cancelled is possessed by the grantor. That is the shape "worker grants the requester a lease over its own attention". In all three uses the document names, the holder is the party doing the work, so its non-renewal releases its tenure, and the work is regranted, that is, continued by someone else. Nothing is cancelled. The illustrated case at lines 795-799, an interpreter that "checks its own tenure at its own points", is a holder. A holder renews from its own control flow, so its bound never passes while it is alive, and no third party can cancel it through non-renewal. The only deliberate cancellation available to the grantor is a reclaim with cause policy, whose `:lapsed` record does not travel (lines 266, 447), so a holder on an in-process medium never learns it was cancelled. Three corrections. First, drop "stronger than a flag": a lease is a flag plus a dead-man's switch, and the flag half already exists and is named `:released`. Second, state the role rule: to be cancellable-on-death, the party whose death should cancel must be the renewer, and the party that stops must be the grantor; the grantor's rule is reclaim, not "stop acting at the bound". Third, the holder's self-check now carries weight it did not before, and line 320's "its own clock" is a host call by the document's own standard at lines 395-398. A portable holder needs the same discipline the judge gets: a tick stream, observation time as most recent tick, and a bound measured in tick time. The vocabulary does not give it that rule.

**HIGH | Renewal is evidence, lines 302-310, against lines 229-232 | "A grantor honours eligible renewals for the granted duration."**
Two earlier paragraphs empty this. Line 229 permits a cap below the duration, which by construction ends tenure before the granted duration is honoured. Line 306 lets the grantor end a lease for "a policy change" with an unconstrained `:cause`, so any refusal to honour is relabelled policy. The obligation as stated is either contradicted or vacuous. Correction: narrow it to what is true. Silence alone will not be judged before duration plus tolerance. The cap and policy are separate terms the holder can read on the grant, and the cause set is closed to the four named. Also the holder's stop-at-bound rule (lines 320-323) ignores the cap: a holder must also stop at grant-observation plus max, or it measures a bound it will never be allowed to reach.

**MEDIUM | Judging lapse and What a lease may never do, lines 547, 626, 667 | "root set" survives the purge.**
Line 521 says the ledger "is not a root set in the collector's sense either." Line 547 then calls it "the judge's own root set", line 626 "so the root set survives", line 667 "A grantor's own root set is not one." The same section disclaims the term and then uses it. Replace all three with "ledger". Related residue at lines 374-377: "conclude about everything before acting on anything, so that an act cannot change what a later conclusion is drawn from" is mark-then-sweep in disguise. Once *now* is pinned for the pass, no act within the pass can change any conclusion, so the ordering carries no weight. Either delete it or say plainly that order within a pass is immaterial because now is fixed.

**MEDIUM | The judgment pass, lines 402-431 | Executable as written, with four holes an implementer hits immediately.**
Granting is not in the pass, yet line 540 says the judge seeds tenure start "from its own act of granting". Under the rule that observation time is the most recent tick, a grant made between passes has no *now* to be stamped with. State that granting happens inside a pass and takes that pass's reading, or that the grant is stamped at the next pass's step 1. Second, the first pass, or any pass, may drain zero ticks; say the judge judges nothing until a first tick exists and otherwise reuses the prior reading. Third, under per-attachment media, which line 607 recommends, "the lease-fact cursor" is N cursors plus the boundary lifecycle stream the WebSocket document requires (ws lines 140-142). Fourth, step 5 must also retry appends for leases already in the pending set, and the action on a backwards tick (line 403) is unstated: ignore it.

**MEDIUM | A lease is facts, lines 186-191 | "A reader of the grant alone must be able to say what was granted and to whom."**
The holder cannot, in the canonical case. `:dao.lease/holder` carries the server-side attachment identity. The WebSocket document says correlation "is added locally, on both ends independently" (ws lines 134-136), so the client's own attachment value is different data. A holder cannot match itself against the holder field. Correction: state that the holder trusts the channel the grant arrived on, not the field, or that the composition supplies the holder its identity as the judge sees it. On a shared in-process medium the second is mandatory.

**MEDIUM | Precedent, lines 82-86 | "Decay is what limits the damage rather than curing it."**
Overstated in the wrong direction. Plain reference counting leaks dead cycles; that is the defect leases were added to fix, and decay cures it completely, since dead parties do not renew. What survives is two live parties holding each other for no reason, which is the renewal-forever case at line 691 and is bounded by max. Correction: say decay eliminates dead cycles and that live mutual tenure is priced by the cap.

**LOW | Judging lapse, lines 526-528 | "or the reclaim that answered a release completed and recorded."**
Step 5 says `:lapsed` is appended in every case, so this alternative exit is obsolete. Delete the clause.

**LOW | Purpose, line 135, against lines 805-807 | "how do I stop caring about something that may never answer."**
The closing section says a lease is the wrong instrument for a one-shot answer that never arrives. Rephrase the purpose to "something that may never speak again".

**LOW | Renewal forever, line 693 | "A hostile holder gains little."**
Lines 603-606 say one flooding attachment on a shared medium keeps every dead holder on that medium alive indefinitely. That is not little. Say the gain is bounded only where media are per-attachment.

**LOW | Precedent, lines 34-36 | "through Network Objects and then RMI's DGC."**
As I recall it, Modula-3 Network Objects supplied the dirty/clean reference count but detected client death with server-initiated pings, and RMI's DGC replaced those pings with Gray-Cheriton leases. Worth checking before the document claims lineage precision, since a lineage error in the correction is what round 3 was fixing.

**LOW | Whose obligation, lines 652-655 | The reclaim bound omits the tick period.**
Every interval is measured in tick time, so the real-time bound adds one tick period and one adapter deposit lag. Add the term, or say the bound is in tick time.

Nothing found at a severity above these regarding invariants against callbacks or hidden state; the anti-registry argument stands without the root-set claim, as round 3 intended.

## Is it finishable?

Yes, but not by a fifth round of the same kind. The trend is real, and this round shows its mechanism. Three of the four high findings are round-3 additions contradicting paragraphs that were already there: honour-granted-time against cap-below-duration, the cap limits paragraph against itself, the root-set disclaimer against three surviving uses. The cancellation section is new prose that reasons about roles the normative text never fixed. Each fix is a local argument appended to an 815-line document that has no normative core separate from its rationale, so every fix argues against the previous argument and nothing checks the rules against each other.

Two changes would stop it. First, split the document. A normative section of perhaps 150 lines: the facts table, the judge's state, the pass, the holder's rules including its clock, the sizing relations, the composition duties. Every sentence a rule, no lineage, no precedent. Rationale moves below it or to a companion. Reviews then test the rules for consistency, and the rationale can be imperfect without breaking anything. Second, walk the two concrete compositions through the normative section end to end, the served connection and the in-process pause, from grant to lapse. Every executability hole above, the grant path, the holder recognizing itself, the holder's clock, the N cursors, would have surfaced from the first walkthrough. Until both are done, no new generalizations should be admitted. The cancellation section is a good idea that arrived before the rules it depends on existed.
